// When a part of a country stops being part of it.
//
// A region does not secede because it is poor, or because it is angry, or because it is
// foreign. It secedes because it is all three at once and because the state holding it has
// stopped looking like it can stop them - which is why this reads four numbers off the
// region and one off the country, and why none of them alone is enough.
//
// Each week a region adds to its pressure:
//
//     base
//   * (foreign_share ^ nationality)    how far the state is not theirs
//   * (radicalism_floor + radicalism * radicalism)
//   * (1 - stability)                  how far the region has stopped being governed
//   * (weakness_floor + weakness * (1 - national stability))
//   * war_multiplier                   while the state is losing a war somewhere
//
// and when a contiguous group of regions that share a people passes 1, they go.
//
// Everything here is deliberately slow. A country coming apart should be something a player
// watches happening and has time to act about - raise living standards, spend stability on
// holding it, win the war that started it - rather than something that happens to them
// between two clicks.
{
  // A week's pressure for a region that is wholly foreign, wholly radical, wholly
  // ungoverned and held by a state with no stability left. Everything real is a fraction of
  // that, so this is a few months for a region that is all four and never for a region that
  // is only one of them.
  //
  // Calibrated against the 2024 world and then against what fifteen years of it actually
  // does, rather than assumed. The worst region on the map is Chechnya: a Russia that
  // governs itself holds it for about sixteen years, a Russia whose stability has slipped
  // holds it for ten, and a Russia losing a war holds it for five. A region that is merely
  // half foreign and reasonably governed is past a century, which is to say never.
  //
  // At this rate the world produces about one and a half new countries a year - India loses
  // Maharashtra, Indonesia loses Kalimantan, Ethiopia loses the Ogaden, Iran and Armenia
  // both lose Azerbaijani ground - which is a map that visibly changes across a long game
  // without becoming unrecognisable in a decade. Double this and it is four a year and the
  // world is gone by 2060.
  "base": 0.009,

  // How sharply being somebody else's people matters. A region that is half the state's
  // own nationality is far less than half as likely to leave.
  "nationality": 2.20,

  // How much of this is about anger rather than nationality.
  //
  // The floor carries most of it, and that is a measurement rather than a preference:
  // region radicalism in a world at peace sits at zero almost everywhere - the median
  // region is 0.000 and the ninety-fifth percentile is 0.004 - because it is fed by
  // minorities under a moved border and by budgets nobody has cut yet. A floor of 0.15
  // therefore made this term a constant, and a constant that quietly divided the whole
  // mechanic by seven. So the floor is what a grievance is worth on its own, and the
  // remaining 0.65 is what radicalising a region on top of that buys - still nearly three
  // times the rate, which is the whole range the stat can actually deliver.
  "radicalism_floor": 0.35,
  "radicalism": 0.65,

  // How much a state that is visibly failing invites it. A settled country loses nothing;
  // one at the floor of its stability loses whatever is least attached to it.
  "weakness_floor": 0.20,
  "weakness": 0.80,

  // A state losing a war is a state that cannot spare a division for a province. Applied
  // only while a war is actually going badly.
  "war_multiplier": 1.80,

  // Below this a region never accumulates anything at all, whatever else is true of it.
  // A region that is half the state's own people is not a secession waiting to happen,
  // however badly it is governed - that is a revolution, and a different mechanic.
  "minimum_foreign_share": 0.55,

  // And a region has to be worth being a country. One province and forty thousand people
  // is a grievance, not a state.
  "minimum_population": 300000,
  "minimum_regions": 1,

  // What goes with them.
  //
  // A secession is not tidy and never has been. There is a core - a people with a grievance,
  // which is everything above - and then there is whatever was next to the core and had
  // nothing left to lose. The second kind is frequently not the same nationality as the
  // first, or anybody: a border moves, and a province that was already angry and already
  // ungoverned simply finds itself on the far side of it and stays there.
  //
  // So the new country grows outward from the core, one region at a time, taking whichever
  // neighbour is least attached. A region joins on either count - it was nearly gone itself,
  // or it is simply discontented enough that nothing holds it.
  "join_pressure": 0.55,
  "join_discontent": 0.55,

  // How many can come along. Six is a country with a shape; no limit is a country that ate
  // half a continent because the week went badly.
  "join_limit": 6,

  // Only the core's own neighbours come along, and only where the seceding people are at least
  // this share of the region (a federal break, about conditions rather than a people, is exempt).
  // Ground that is another country's core never comes along: that is a different war.
  "join_people_share": 0.20,

  // And the hard cap. Past this share of the parent's people the joiners are handed back,
  // largest first, until what leaves is a secession rather than a dissolution. The core is
  // never handed back: a people leaving is the thing that happened, whatever share of the
  // country it turns out to be.
  "maximum_share": 0.40,

  // What the new country starts with.
  //
  // A state born out of a grievance is not a state with an army, but it is a state whose
  // people have just decided something - and that is exactly the condition the militia
  // rules read. It starts with these formations outright and raises more the ordinary way
  // if anybody comes for it.
  "militia_formations": 4,

  // Where it starts politically. Stability is low because everything is new; war support is
  // high because it has just been declared.
  "stability": 0.42,
  "war_support": 0.80,

  // How likely the state it left is to simply accept it, before anything else is
  // considered. The rest is worked out from the state of the country - see SecessionCore.
  //
  // Low, and it has to be: nearly every country this happens to is a country whose stability
  // has already collapsed, and the stability term alone pushes acceptance towards two in
  // three. At 0.25 the world let nine secessions in ten go peacefully, which made the whole
  // "or fight about it" half of the mechanic something a player would rarely see.
  "acceptance": 0.10,

  // What "cannot fight about it" actually means.
  //
  // Instability used to buy acceptance across the whole range, which had the model
  // backwards - the state a region leaves is by construction an unstable one, so the
  // exception swallowed the rule and nine secessions in ten were waved through. A state
  // whose grip is slipping is the one most likely to shoot. What it lacks is the ability to
  // win, and that only really goes at collapse: below `collapse_below` there is no
  // instrument left to send, and that is worth `collapse` towards letting go.
  "collapse_below": 0.30,
  "collapse": 0.30,

  // An army in being is an argument for using it. Full weight at this many divisions.
  "army_resolve": 0.22,
  "army_resolve_divisions": 24,

  // And how long before anybody else can leave the same country - counted from either side,
  // so a country three weeks old is not yet somewhere anybody secedes from either.
  //
  // Two years rather than six months. A state does not come apart in four pieces on the same
  // afternoon, and the shorter figure let Indonesia shed a country every winter. The regions
  // waiting keep everything they have accumulated: the first thing that happens when the two
  // years are up is the next one leaving, which is what a country genuinely coming apart
  // looks like.
  "cooldown_days": 730
}
